# session.manifest — config owned by mod_session (session memory: windows,
# workspaces, focus).
#
# Three layers, two of them tracked here (update-system.md phase 2):
#
#   • /usr/share/w/defaults/session.conf + .schema — W's defaults and WHO may
#     set each key. Shipped data, class managed: W owns them, every update
#     overwrites.
#   • /usr/share/w/defaults/session-apps.tsv — the shipped relaunch overrides
#     (notably: terminals restore through `w-term`, not through whichever binary
#     they happened to be). Same class, same reason.
#   • /usr/share/w/defaults/session-terminal.list — which programs running INSIDE
#     a terminal are safe to re-execute at login. Same class again: it is a
#     safety default W owns and improves, not a per-machine choice.
#   • /etc/w/session.conf — the admin's DEVIATIONS from those defaults. Class
#     override: the update never touches it; `w-reset session` drops the
#     deviations so the machine falls back to the vendor layer.
#
# ~/.config/w/session.conf is where the settings normally land (every key is
# user-scope), created on demand and with no vendor default — class user-conf:
# `w-reset session` deletes it (its "default" IS absence). Its sibling
# ~/.config/w/session-apps.tsv is the user's own relaunch table, and
# ~/.config/w/session-terminal.list their own additions to the allowlist — same
# class and the same reasoning.
#
# NOT tracked here — runtime state, not configuration:
#   • ~/.local/state/w/session.json — the snapshot itself. It is not a setting
#     and it has no vendor version to restore from; what deletes it is
#     `w-session forget`, which is the operation that means it. Listing it as
#     user-conf would make `w-reset session` silently throw away the saved
#     session as a side effect of resetting a preference — the same conflation
#     the four reset categories exist to avoid.
#   • /etc/systemd/user/w-session-{save,restore}.service — units, deployed by
#     apply_rootfs and enabled by mod_session, not user-editable config.
#
# Format: <path>\t<class>   class: managed | override | user-conf
/usr/share/w/defaults/session.conf	managed
/usr/share/w/defaults/session.schema	managed
/usr/share/w/defaults/session-apps.tsv	managed
/usr/share/w/defaults/session-terminal.list	managed
/etc/w/session.conf	override
.config/w/session.conf	user-conf
.config/w/session-apps.tsv	user-conf
.config/w/session-terminal.list	user-conf
